---
<Layout
  title="The Authorial Capacity Constraint"
  description="Working Paper 001 v0.3: a lineage-corrected synthesis of AI-assisted performance, human defensibility, contribution, discovery attribution and epistemic stewardship."
>
  <header class="page-head paper-head">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">Working Paper 001 · v0.3 · 7 September 2026</p>
      <h1>The Authorial Capacity Constraint</h1>
      <p class="lede">Epistemic stewardship under cognitive abundance.</p>
      <div class="paper-meta" aria-label="Publication status">
        <span>Exploratory</span>
        <span>Machine-assisted</span>
        <span>Lineage reviewed</span>
        <span>Not peer reviewed</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">v0.3 correction</p>
        <h2>This paper no longer implies that it discovered the underlying problem.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="large">The prior-art review found close work that materially narrows the contribution claimed here.</p>
        <p>Research on AI-assisted metacognition, academic verification gaps and claim accountability already describes important parts of the separation between artefact quality, human understanding and defensibility. Longer-standing work on epistemic dependence, contributorship and computational opacity provides further foundations.</p>
        <div class="callout"><p><strong>Finding prior art changes the claim. It does not become a footnote added to preserve the old claim.</strong></p></div>
        <p>This version therefore treats <strong>Authorial Capacity Constraint</strong> as a local organizing label and synthesis. Its possible contribution lies in linking existing concerns to claim-sensitive authority, contribution provenance, epistemic handoff, uptake scarcity, standards-compatible provenance and the economics of epistemic abundance.</p>
        <p><a href={internal('lineage')}>Read the full Intellectual Lineage & Novelty Register →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Epistemic status</p>
        <h2>This paper begins with a limitation.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="large">The sophistication of this text may exceed the present ability of its human curator to defend every argument it contains.</p>
        <p>That limitation does not confer novelty on the text and does not dissolve responsibility. It motivates a research problem: machine assistance can improve an intellectual artefact without proportionately improving the human participant's understanding, calibration or capacity to defend the resulting claims.</p>
        <div class="callout"><p><strong>Epistemic authority should not exceed defensibility.</strong></p></div>
        <p class="muted">The sentence is retained as an Agalmic Research operating maxim. It should not be read as a claim that this project first discovered claim-level defensibility as a scholarly norm.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="paper-body">
    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">Abstract</p>
        <p class="paper-abstract">Generative AI can improve the quality and sophistication of an intellectual artefact without proportionately improving the human user's understanding, metacognitive calibration or ability to defend the claims it contains. This paper calls that practical configuration the <strong>Authorial Capacity Constraint</strong>, while explicitly acknowledging that the underlying phenomenon has close antecedents in work on AI-assisted metacognition, verification gaps, claim accountability, epistemic dependence, contributorship and computational opacity. The paper's proposed contribution is therefore a synthesis and research architecture rather than a claim of first discovery. It separates contribution provenance from claim-level epistemic authority; treats low authority as a possible trigger for <strong>epistemic handoff</strong>; connects the resulting evaluation burden to absorptive capacity and the <strong>economics of epistemic abundance</strong>; and proposes standards-first provenance using W3C PROV, CRediT and RO-Crate. It also introduces an open <strong>Discovery Attribution Problem</strong>: when a machine generates a candidate while humans or other systems direct the search, recognize significance, validate, integrate and realize it, does the machine discover knowledge or facilitate a distributed discovery? Novelty of the result is treated as distinct from the attribution of discovery.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">1 · Intellectual lineage</p>
        <h2>The problem has ancestors, including very close ones.</h2>
        <p>Several bodies of work substantially predate this paper. Hardwig's account of <em>epistemic dependence</em> makes clear that modern knowers routinely rely on expertise they do not personally possess. Humphreys' work on computational science develops <em>epistemic opacity</em>: reliable computational processes may outrun the ability of a human to survey every epistemically relevant step.</p>
        <p>Scientific publishing has also long recognized that authorship compresses heterogeneous contributions. The BMJ's contributorship and guarantorship reforms separated "who did what" from responsibility for the work, while the later CRediT taxonomy standardized fourteen contributor roles.</p>
        <p>The closest AI-era antecedents are more direct. Fernandes and colleagues report that LLM assistance can improve reasoning performance without comparably improving metacognitive accuracy. Maier develops an explanatory framework in which improved AI-assisted outcomes can coexist with weak self-evaluation of what the user actually understands or can perform independently. O'Keefe's <em>Verification Gap</em> explicitly describes a widening gap between artefact quality and demonstrated understanding in education.</p>
        <p>Most importantly for this paper's normative framing, van Zoonen, Morgan-Thomas and Tursunbayeva argue that AI governance in publishing should move from tool disclosure to <strong>claim accountability</strong>: a named human should be positioned to reconstruct and defend the claims entering the scholarly record. Nascimento and da Silva separately emphasize that identical AI-assisted errors can carry different epistemic responsibility depending on the diligence exercised by the human author.</p>
        <div class="callout"><p><strong>Accordingly, this paper does not claim novelty for the proposition that AI-assisted output can exceed human understanding, or for the proposition that scholarly claims require accountable defence.</strong></p></div>
        <p>The research question is what follows when those observations are combined with granular contribution provenance, explicit handoff to missing expertise and the economics of a much larger supply of candidate knowledge.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">2 · The practical constraint</p>
        <h2>Production capacity and stewardship capacity can diverge.</h2>
        <p><strong>Authorial Capacity Constraint:</strong> in machine-assisted knowledge production, the sophistication or apparent quality of an artefact can exceed the human curator's capacity to understand, interrogate, verify and appropriately qualify its claims.</p>
        <p>The term is useful only if it does more than rename the verification gap. Here it is used as a bridge between three levels that should be kept separate:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Artefact validity:</strong> is the claim, proof, analysis or result actually warranted?</li>
          <li><strong>Human comprehension:</strong> what can the named human reconstruct, explain, transfer and diagnose?</li>
          <li><strong>Epistemic accountability:</strong> which person, system or institution can responsibly answer for the claim, and on what basis?</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Those variables can diverge. A theorem can be correct while the human presenting it cannot follow the proof. A researcher can understand an argument deeply and still be wrong. Perfect provenance can show where a claim came from without making the claim true.</p>
        <p>That decomposition is more useful than a single "AI use" disclosure because it exposes what kind of missing warrant must be supplied.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">3 · Metacognition, not the Dunning–Kruger caricature</p>
        <h2>The relevant problem is calibration.</h2>
        <p>Kruger and Dunning's 1999 experiments are historically relevant to the idea that task competence and self-assessment can be linked. But the popular version of the "Dunning–Kruger effect" is too blunt to ground this argument, and later work has challenged the magnitude and interpretation of common demonstrations.</p>
        <p>The stronger foundation is empirical work on AI-assisted calibration. Fernandes et al. find that improved LLM-assisted performance did not scaffold metacognitive monitoring. Maier's framework likewise distinguishes assisted performance from understanding, learning and accurate self-evaluation.</p>
        <p>The paper therefore requires only a modest premise: <strong>quality of assisted output is not a reliable measure of the user's independent understanding of that output.</strong></p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">4 · Defensible stewardship</p>
        <h2>A local operating maxim built on claim accountability.</h2>
        <div class="thesis paper-thesis">
          <p class="eyebrow">Operating maxim</p>
          <blockquote>Epistemic authority should not exceed defensibility.</blockquote>
          <p>Where a human cannot directly defend a claim, the public representation of authority should reflect whatever alternative warrant actually exists: expert review, empirical evidence, formal proof, independent reproduction, transparent provenance or other reliable mechanisms.</p>
        </div>
        <p>This is closely aligned with van Zoonen et al.'s claim-accountability framework and with older guarantorship ideas. The distinction proposed here is not a superior slogan. It is the attempt to connect those norms to a machine-readable research object in which different claims can carry different forms of authority.</p>
        <p>A curator need not derive everything unaided. Distributed cognition is normal science. The failure occurs when dependency is hidden or when an artefact's polish is allowed to imply a level of personal mastery that the contributor has not demonstrated.</p>
        <p><strong>The right to publish need not require mastery. A claim of mastery requires warrant.</strong></p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">5 · Contribution without inventing another taxonomy</p>
        <h2>Use CRediT first. Add only what it cannot express.</h2>
        <p>Version 0.2 proposed a local list of roles such as initiator, framer, generator, selector, verifier and realizer. That list was useful for thinking, but it risks duplicating decades of contributorship work.</p>
        <p>Version 0.3 therefore adopts a standards-first rule. Where a contribution maps to the ANSI/NISO <strong>CRediT</strong> taxonomy, use the standard role: Conceptualization, Methodology, Investigation, Formal analysis, Validation, Software, Visualization, Writing – original draft, Writing – review & editing, Project administration and the other defined roles.</p>
        <p>Fine-grained local descriptors may remain where they preserve information CRediT does not distinguish well, for example an <em>initiator</em> versus a later conceptual developer, a human <em>selector</em> choosing among machine-generated candidates, or a <em>domain steward</em> who accepts responsibility for a specific claim cluster. Such terms are secondary descriptors, not a rival taxonomy.</p>
        <div class="callout"><p><strong>Origin deserves credit. Authority requires warrant.</strong></p></div>
        <p>The useful extension is therefore not "contribution roles exist." It is that contribution roles and <strong>claim-level epistemic authority</strong> should be maintained as separate records.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">6 · Discovery Attribution Problem</p>
        <h2>Does the machine discover, or does it facilitate discovery?</h2>
        <p>Machine-assisted research creates a harder attribution problem than authorship alone. Suppose a human poses an unusual question, directs a search and recognizes the significance of one candidate among thousands, while a model generates the candidate. Another model or proof checker validates it. A domain expert later integrates it into the literature. Who discovered the knowledge?</p>
        <p>The question should not be answered by assigning the whole episode to whichever actor produced the final sentence. At least five events should be distinguished:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Candidate generation:</strong> producing or surfacing a proposition, proof, mechanism, design or connection.</li>
          <li><strong>Recognition:</strong> identifying that a candidate is meaningful, anomalous, surprising or worth pursuing.</li>
          <li><strong>Validation:</strong> supplying proof, evidence, replication or expert scrutiny.</li>
          <li><strong>Integration:</strong> locating the warranted result in the existing body of knowledge, including prior art.</li>
          <li><strong>Realization:</strong> turning the result into a usable scientific, technical, institutional or economic object.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Current debate does not settle whether present AI systems are themselves epistemic agents or knowers. Nature Methods has explicitly asked what happens when hypothesis generation is outsourced to machines, while AI co-scientist systems increasingly generate and test scientific hypotheses. Other work argues that current generative AI remains dependent on human knowledge spaces and struggles with genuinely fundamental discovery. The philosophical question should therefore remain open.</p>
        <p>For novelty assessment, however, one conclusion is clearer:</p>
        <div class="thesis paper-thesis">
          <p class="eyebrow">Novelty discipline</p>
          <blockquote>Novelty belongs to the result relative to prior knowledge, not to the subjective surprise of the actor who encountered it.</blockquote>
          <p>If a model surfaces an existing idea unknown to the curator, the event may be an independent rediscovery but the result is not new. If a genuinely unprecedented result emerges from machine-assisted search, novelty and discovery credit remain separate questions.</p>
        </div>
        <p>This matters reflexively. A language model can make an old intellectual wheel feel newly discovered because neither participant has yet retrieved the literature around it. Recognition of significance is real intellectual work, but it is not a substitute for a prior-art search.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">7 · Epistemic handoff</p>
        <h2>Low authority can trigger routing rather than erasure.</h2>
        <p>A person can make a useful contribution without possessing the expertise needed to validate it. This observation has important antecedents in distributed expertise, knowledge brokering and contributorship. The proposed <strong>epistemic handoff</strong> is a narrower institutional pattern: preserve contribution provenance, expose the missing authority, and route the candidate toward actors capable of supplying the missing epistemic function.</p>
        <p>A handoff might move a conceptual insight to a mathematician for formalization, a statistical claim to a methodologist, an empirical conjecture to an experimental group, or a machine-generated proof to independent formal verification.</p>
        <p>The handoff should add warrant without rewriting who contributed the earlier work. Conversely, early contribution should not be used to claim expertise acquired only later from validators.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">8 · Economics of epistemic abundance</p>
        <h2>Uptake has a lineage too.</h2>
        <p>The economic part of this argument has strong antecedents. Herbert Simon's information-rich world produces a scarcity of attention. Weitzman's recombinant-growth model warns that processing abundant possible ideas into usable form can itself become limiting. Cohen and Levinthal's <strong>absorptive capacity</strong> concerns the ability to recognize the value of external information, assimilate it and apply it. Teece shows that value can migrate toward complementary assets when imitation is easy.</p>
        <p>Accordingly, the project no longer presents "epistemic uptake" as a discovery from first principles. It is an AI-era extension question:</p>
        <div class="callout"><p><strong>What happens to absorptive, evaluative and realization capacity when the supply of plausible candidate knowledge grows much faster than traditional institutions were designed to receive?</strong></p></div>
        <p>The broader branch is now called <strong>Economics of Epistemic Abundance</strong>, both because that phrase better describes the specific problem and because "Epistemic Economics" already names Anna Grandori's substantial prior programme.</p>
        <p>The proposed <strong>Displaced Scarcity Hypothesis</strong> is likewise downgraded from "law": when a previously binding constraint becomes sufficiently abundant, performance and value may become increasingly determined by complementary constraints that remain scarce. Whether this is a useful generalization beyond established bottleneck and complementarity theory remains to be shown.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">9 · Standards-first research provenance</p>
        <h2>The Memory Palace should not become another provenance standard.</h2>
        <p>W3C PROV already provides a general model for entities, activities, agents, attribution, derivation and responsibility. RO-Crate already packages research objects with machine-readable JSON-LD metadata. CRediT already standardizes scholarly contribution roles. MIRA already demonstrates a graph of questions, claims and evidence with granular attribution.</p>
        <p>The Agalmic Research Memory Palace is therefore being repositioned as an <strong>application profile and extension</strong> over existing systems. Ordinary provenance should use the mature standards. Local metadata is reserved for the research questions they do not directly answer:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>claim-level epistemic status and authority basis;</li>
          <li>explicit epistemic handoff;</li>
          <li>search-space branch state;</li>
          <li>roads not taken and surfaced-but-unexamined alternatives;</li>
          <li>search boundaries;</li>
          <li>reversal conditions for consequential research decisions.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The repository now includes an experimental RO-Crate metadata document and a W3C PROV-compatible JSON-LD projection. These are beginnings, not a claim of standards conformance beyond what has actually been implemented.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">10 · Research programme</p>
        <h2>The surviving questions should become testable.</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>How large is the gap between AI-assisted artefact quality and demonstrated human understanding across domains?</li>
          <li>Which tests measure defensibility without merely measuring rhetorical fluency or memory?</li>
          <li>Can claim-level authority metadata predict expert judgments of reliability or appropriate reliance?</li>
          <li>When can formal verification or independent reproduction compensate for limited human comprehension?</li>
          <li>Does explicit handoff improve the rate at which useful low-authority contributions are validated without increasing appropriation?</li>
          <li>How does machine-generated candidate abundance change organizational or societal absorptive capacity?</li>
          <li>Which contribution events deserve credit in machine-assisted discovery: generation, recognition, validation, integration, realization, or some combination?</li>
          <li>Can W3C PROV, RO-Crate and CRediT represent enough of this process that local extensions remain genuinely small?</li>
          <li>What evidence would distinguish a non-trivial Displaced Scarcity Hypothesis from ordinary bottleneck reasoning?</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">11 · Deep future</p>
        <h2>When reliable knowledge exceeds human-scale comprehension.</h2>
        <p>This conjecture also has substantial intellectual ancestors. Humphreys' computational-opacity work and the epistemology of computer-assisted proof already examine knowledge supported by processes humans cannot survey end to end. Recent philosophy of mathematics asks whether opaque AI systems plus proof checking can yield mathematical knowledge even when generated proofs are not humanly surveyable, and recent work on mathematical intuition discusses proof production separating from later human "digestion".</p>
        <div class="thesis paper-thesis">
          <p class="eyebrow">Speculative label · Human-Scale Epistemic Horizon</p>
          <blockquote>There may be domains in which the frontier of reliable knowledge advances beyond the point at which any individual human can personally comprehend and defend the best available results.</blockquote>
          <p>The label is not offered as discovery of epistemic opacity. The open question is what happens institutionally and economically if opacity becomes normal rather than exceptional at a knowledge frontier.</p>
        </div>
        <p>Mathematics remains a useful test case because formal verification allows correctness and human intelligibility to separate unusually cleanly. If frontier proofs became machine-generated, machine-checkable and humanly unsurveyable, defensibility might migrate from reconstructing the proof to defending the verification architecture, assumptions, independent checks and known failure modes.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">12 · Reflexive application</p>
        <h2>Agalmic Research is exposed to the exact failure it studies.</h2>
        <p>This programme uses large language models extensively for search, synthesis, drafting and criticism. That makes independent rediscovery particularly easy: a coherent model-generated synthesis can feel intellectually novel before either participant has mapped the relevant literature.</p>
        <p>The project's response is now procedural. Before a concept enters the numbered paper sequence, it receives an intellectual-lineage classification. Provenance uses mature standards where possible. When a close antecedent is found, the claim is narrowed or renamed. Working Paper 001 v0.3 is itself the first substantial correction produced by that process.</p>
        <p>The human curator initiated the central concerns that led to this paper, including discomfort about limited capacity to defend machine-assisted work, the distinction between idea initiators and those with epistemic authority, and the present question of whether machine cognition discovers knowledge or facilitates discovery through human direction and recognition. ChatGPT materially contributed literature search, terminology, synthesis, criticism and extensive drafting.</p>
        <p>Those contribution facts are not claims that either participant has established the paper's central propositions as true or novel.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">13 · Limitations</p>
        <h2>What v0.3 still does not establish.</h2>
        <p>The current prior-art review is broad rather than systematic. It does not exhaust Scopus, Web of Science, EconLit, PhilPapers, ProQuest, patent databases or non-English literature. The precise novelty of the local synthesis therefore remains unassessed.</p>
        <p>No validated metric of defensibility, comprehension, epistemic authority, handoff quality or uptake capacity is supplied. The proposed authority layer may prove cumbersome, gameable or redundant with existing scholarly practices. The distinction between machine generation and discovery remains philosophically unresolved.</p>
        <p>The Displaced Scarcity Hypothesis is not a law. The Human-Scale Epistemic Horizon is a speculative label over an established family of opacity problems. The Memory Palace extension has not yet been reviewed by W3C PROV, RO-Crate or scholarly-metadata experts.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell paper-section">
        <p class="eyebrow">AI-assistance & contribution declaration</p>
        <h2>How this version was produced.</h2>
        <p><strong>Human contribution:</strong> Conceptualization, search direction, selection and publication decisions. The human curator initiated the concern about authorial defensibility, insisted that it become the first paper, introduced the separation between idea initiation and epistemic authority, and raised the Discovery Attribution Problem concerning machine generation versus human direction and recognition.</p>
        <p><strong>Machine contribution:</strong> OpenAI ChatGPT materially contributed investigation/literature search, conceptual synthesis, terminology, criticism, structure, original drafting and revision. These descriptions are mapped where possible to CRediT-style roles, while finer process events remain in the provenance record.</p>
        <p><strong>Authority:</strong> neither contribution statement establishes domain expertise or independent validation of the paper. Relevant claims require further handoff to scholars in metacognition, epistemology, philosophy of science, scholarly communication, economics/innovation studies and provenance standards.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

